          "case_name": "Peer News LLC v. City & County of Honolulu",
          "citation": "143 Haw. 472, 431 P.3d 1245 (2018)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2018,
          "date_decided": "2018-12-21",
          "holding": "The court held that the Office of Information Practices palpably erred in reading a categorical deliberative process privilege into HRS § 92F-13(3), because that exception requires an individualized showing that disclosure would frustrate a legitimate government function.",
          "relevance": "Audit and budget-related internal records are not categorically exempt from a UIPA request.",
          "quote": "OIP palpably erred in adopting a deliberative process privilege pursuant to the HRS § 92F-13(3) exception for documents that would frustrate a legitimate government function if disclosed",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4576246/peer-news-llc-v-city-of-honolulu-dept-of-budget-fiscal-servs/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4576246
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Honolulu Civil Beat Inc. v. Department of the Attorney General",
          "citation": "146 Haw. 285, 463 P.3d 942 (2020)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2020,
          "date_decided": "2020-03-11",
          "holding": "The court applied the UIPA's presumption of openness in reviewing the Attorney General's refusal to release an investigative report, holding that the agency bears the burden of establishing that a claimed exemption, including one resting on privilege, actually applies.",
          "relevance": "Audit and investigative reports prepared for a state agency are presumptively disclosable.",
          "quote": "The UIPA contains a strong presumption in favor of public disclosure of government records.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4734772/honolulu-civil-beat-inc-v-department-of-the-attorney-general/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4734772
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Public First Law Center v. Viola",
          "citation": "No. SCPW-24-0000464 (Haw. Sept. 30, 2025)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2025,
          "date_decided": "2025-09-30",
          "holding": "In an original proceeding arising from the death of foster child Isabella Kalua, the court held that the 'legitimate purpose' standard of HRS § 587A-40 for disclosing confidential Child Protective Act records is not limited to the child's best interests, that a legitimate purpose and 'good cause' under HRS § 578-15 exist to release CPA and connected adoption records where a foster child is missing, has suffered a near fatality, been critically injured, or has died, and it granted the petition and ordered disclosure with redactions protecting the surviving siblings.",
          "relevance": "Financial and programmatic scrutiny of how DHS handled a particular case is possible because the underlying case records are disclosable on a legitimate-purpose showing rather than sealed as a class.",
          "quote": "We hold that the standard for whether a legitimate purpose exists for disclosure of CPA records is not grounded solely in the best interest of the child standard.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10682020/public-first-law-center-v-viola/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 10682020
        }
